\subsection{Causal planar operation calculus and the remaining cell obligation}
\label{app:planar-causal-calculus}
\textbf{PROVED --- analytical (operation identities); invariant cell OPEN.}
These identities supply the linear term and the finite nonlinear remainder in
$\delta z^+=A\delta z+N(\delta z)+d$; they do not supply an empirical
contraction constant. All maps below are real lifts of the literal source
operations on the stated branch, with arithmetic charged separately.

\paragraph{Causal state and parity.}
Write $e=(q_y,b_{g,y},v_{xz},p_{xz},S_{xz},a_{w,xz},b_{a,xz})$ for the
12 local mean coordinates (the attitude-error slot is reset after injection).
On the planar regular branch the other nine mean coordinates vanish, while
\[
 P=P_E\oplus P_O,\qquad \dim P_E=12,\quad\dim P_O=9.
\]
Let $\eta$ contain the raw private Mahony quaternion and integral, every guard
LP/detector/weight state, band/frequency/variance/period statistics, staged
and committed tuner values, internally filtered model/noise parameters,
$S$ elapsed time and period, and AW time, pending flag and target.
Let $\chi$ contain the startup stage, saved sensor samples, gravity/watchdog
states, magnetic acquisition/refinement and continuous hard-iron sufficient
statistics, reference, applied offset, and all validity/solver/hold flags.
The source phase and magnetic packet residue are $\phi$ and $k\bmod8$ for the
stated $200$ Hz/$25$ Hz record. Absolute implementation clocks remain in the
state: they are not replaced by a periodic phase if their arithmetic matters.
The source-bound causal state is $(\phi,\eta,\chi,e,P_E,P_O)$.

The Complementary tuner/S-period/AW-target generator has no reverse edge
from $(e,P)$; this statement does not delete reference or gate feedback.
On a fixed regular parity-preserving branch, $r_y=0$, and $K r$ uses only
$P_E$. Thus $(e,P_E,\chi)$ drives $P_O$ without a return through the regular
nonzero mean correction. Solver failures, watchdog resets and reference/gate
branches must still be carried and enclosed. This is a reduction of an
invariant manifold, not permission to delete odd perturbations or the
physical compatibility gauge. Both covariance blocks are necessary for
magnetic service. The branch requires planar initialization/reference and the
stated zero-lever-arm/deheel profile; no assertion is made that an arbitrary
initial state lies on it.

\paragraph{A real-arithmetic reference bound from the same history.}
The specified profile leaves startup hard-iron estimation disabled. In the
continuous estimator let $w$ be its weight and $A$ its weighted mean of
normalized private tilt rotations. Every such planar rotation fixes $e_y$
on both sides; the literal common forgetting recurrence preserves
$Ae_y=A^Te_y=e_y$. Thus $(I-A^TA)e_y=0$ and the un-ridged minimum-information
gate fails in exact real arithmetic: its information is zero, below the
unchanged positive threshold. The applied offset remains zero. At acquisition
or refinement the learned mean is a convex weighted mean of rotations of the
same norm-$75$ record; canonicalizing it to $(\sqrt{m_x^2+m_y^2},0,m_z)$
preserves its norm. Hence every such committed reference has
$\|B_{\rm ref}\|\le75$. This is an analytical upper bound, not a replay
value. It does not prove the required reference alignment/lower geometry,
finite completion of acquisition/refinement, or float32 transfer. Those
remain in the inherited cell obligation.

\paragraph{Literal event maps.}
The IMU ordering is previous staged-tune commit, clock increments, guard,
private Mahony, noise inflation, MEKF prediction, pending AW synchronization,
due $S=0$ correction, accelerometer correction, watchdog, band/tuner update,
AW-target staging, and period/direction update. The outer wrapper then updates
the gravity/startup state; a delivered magnetic packet runs acquisition,
refinement and offset/reference logic before an eligible Live correction.
Pre-Live updates keep the MEKF held until literal handoff. In particular,
$S$ is not reset in the physical history.

For prediction, let $W=[\omega]_{\times}$ with
$\omega=u_g-\operatorname{proj}_{BG}(b_g)$ and $h$ the supplied step.
The source matrices use
\[
 R_h=I-hg_0W+h^2g_1W^2,\quad
 B_h=hI-h^2g_1W+h^3g_2W^2,\quad
 IB_h=\tfrac12h^2I-h^3g_2W+h^4g_3W^2.
\]
The $g_i$ are the actual finite-polynomial branches for $|h\omega|<1$ and
literal trigonometric branches otherwise, not substituted exact exponentials.
Together with the literal integrated-OU $F_{LL}$ and process $Q$, these give
$P^-=FPF^T+Q$; the BA multiplier is $1$ when held and the shipping OU
multiplier when active. Nominal $(v,p,S,a_w)$ uses its same integrated OU
map. Quaternion prediction left-multiplies by the source's normalized
increment at $-h\omega$. At $\|d\|<.01$ that increment is
\[
 p(d)=\left(1-s/8+s^2/384,
 (1/2-s/48+s^2/3840)d\right),\quad s=d^Td,\quad Q(d)=p/\|p\|.
\]
The other branch uses $(\cos(\sqrt s/2),\sin(\sqrt s/2)d/\sqrt s)$ and
normalizes it. Literal casts/FMA/rounding have separate defects.

For the default zero-lever-arm profile the measurement predictions are
\[
 h_a=R(q)(a_w-ge_z)+b_a,\qquad h_m=R(q)B_{\rm ref},\qquad h_S=S.
\]
Temperature terms, when present, are the same literal known input terms.
For active A21 let $H=Dh$, $S_i=HPH^T+R_i$, $B=PH^T$ and $K=BS_i^{-1}$.
The literal correction is $x_c=x+Kr$ and
$C=P-KB^T-BK^T+KS_iK^T$, followed by quaternion injection, mean projections
and $P^+=GCG^T$, where $G_\theta=I+[d\theta_{\rm inj}]_\times/2$.
In H18, distinguish $H_g$ in $B=M P H_g^T$ from $H_s$ in
$S_i=H_sPH_s^T+R_i$. For held accelerometer updates, $H_g$ omits BA columns,
$M$ masks BA rows, and $H_s$ retains the BA uncertainty and cross terms.
The gain is still $BS_i^{-1}$ and the literal Joseph expression still applies.
The regular unmasked optimal-covariance identity must not be used here.
A taken LDLT diagonal bump is included in $S_i$; a failed solve is its literal
rejection branch. Successful factorization alone is not an SPD proof.

The pending AW map is
\[
 P^+=P+E_A(\Sigma_A-E_A^TPE_A)_+E_A^T,
\]
with the same staged target. It clears the pending flag in the literal order.
The progress-preserving period update maps the inherited elapsed/period pair
by the shipping rescaling and due-event rule; an event is determined from
that state, never selected independently from a phase interval.
All remaining $\eta,\chi$ events are the corresponding named source methods
with their complete sufficient statistics and branches retained.

\paragraph{A causal center, without future observed operands.}
For an inherited center state $\bar z_{k_0}$ define
\[
 \bar z_{k+1}=\mathcal F^{\rm real}_{\bar\sigma_k}(\bar z_k,u(\phi_k)),
 \qquad \bar\sigma_k=\operatorname{branch}(\bar z_k,u(\phi_k)).
\]
Every $\bar H,\bar K,\bar G$, reference and target is evaluated from the
current center state in source order. The one-way generator may be shared
exactly when comparing MEKF perturbations of the same represented sensor
history; then its clock/target differences are zero. For real-versus-float
comparison its arithmetic differences must instead be enclosed. No future
observed coefficient is an input. This defines a causal skew-product center
and proves its well-definedness on regular branches by induction; it does not
prove a compact forward domain or a service floor. A $20$-second return is
neither required nor asserted. The observed-coefficient center remains a
separate FINITE DIAGNOSTIC ONLY artifact.

\paragraph{Complete linked differentials.}
On a fixed branch,
\[
\begin{split}
 dS_i&=dH\,PH^T+H\,dP\,H^T+HP\,dH^T+dR_i,\\
 dK&=(dP\,H^T+P\,dH^T-K\,dS_i)S_i^{-1},\\
 dC&=A\,dP\,A^T-K\,dH\,C-C\,dH^T K^T+K\,dR_i\,K^T,
 \quad A=I-KH,\ C=AP,\\
 dx_c&=dx+dK\,r+K\,dr.
\end{split}
\]
The $dC$ formula is only the unmasked optimal branch. In H18 differentiate
$P-KB^T-BK^T+KS_iK^T$ term by term with
$dB=M(dP\,H_g^T+P\,dH_g^T)$ and $dK=(dB-KdS_i)S_i^{-1}$.
Thus $dR_i$ is not silently zero when the frontend/noise state varies.
If comparing only MEKF perturbations with a shared one-way generator,
the corresponding generated $dR_i$ is exactly zero by causality, not by an
independent extremization.

For $f=R(q)(a_w-ge_z)$ in the left chart,
$df=-[f]_\times d\theta+R\,da_w$ and $dr_a=-df-db_a$ for a fixed sample.
Hence $dH_{a,\theta}=-[df]_\times$, $dH_{a,aw}=[d\theta]_\times R$.
Similarly $dm=-[m]_\times d\theta+R\,dB_{\rm ref}$,
$dH_{m,\theta}=-[dm]_\times$, and $dr_m=-dm$ plus any same-history
applied-offset variation. In particular $dr\ne-dH\,x$.
If $\|a_w\|\le\widehat A$ and $\|B_{\rm ref}\|\le B_*$ on a cell,
\[
 \|dH_a\|\le\sqrt{(g+\widehat A)^2+1}\,\|d\theta\|+\|da_w\|,
 \qquad \|dH_m\|\le B_*\|d\theta\|+\|dB_{\rm ref}\|.
\]
The $+1$ retains the varying rotation block. Physical acceleration amplitude
is not a bound on nominal $a_w$, and physical field norm is not automatically
a bound on the learned reference. If $P\succeq0$ and $R_i\succeq rI>0$,
$\|S_i^{-1}\|\le1/r$ analytically. A carried point innovation floor is not
used as a cell bound.

There is an exact finite gain remainder. Write $\Delta S_i=S_1+S_2$, where
$S_1$ is the displayed differential with finite increments and
\[
 S_2=\Delta H\Delta P H^T+H\Delta P\Delta H^T+
       \Delta H P\Delta H^T+\Delta H\Delta P\Delta H^T.
\]
If $DK=(\Delta P H^T+P\Delta H^T-KS_1)S_i^{-1}$, then
\[
 \Delta K-DK=(\Delta P\Delta H^T-KS_2-DK\Delta S_i)
                   (S_i+\Delta S_i)^{-1}.
\]
Proof: multiply $K+\Delta K$ by $S_i+\Delta S_i$, subtract $KS_i=PH^T$,
and then subtract $DK(S_i+\Delta S_i)$. Thus, for a proven inverse bound $s_*$,
\[
 \|\Delta K-DK\|\le s_*\|
       \Delta P\Delta H^T-KS_2-DK\Delta S_i\|.
\]
This keeps the linked numerator before taking a norm. For any two linearized
increments, the missing bilinear covariance/row/noise interactions are present.

Prediction has
$dP^-=dF\,PF^T+F\,dP\,F^T+FP\,dF^T+dQ$.
Here $dW=-[db_g]_\times$ after the appropriate projection differential;
for example
\[
 dR_h=-h(dg_0 W+g_0dW)+h^2(dg_1W^2+g_1(dW W+W dW)).
\]
The $dg_i$, $dF_{LL}$ and $dQ$ follow the same finite polynomial/exponential
source branches and their current parameter state; they are not frozen in a
complete derivative. For the small quaternion branch, $ds=2d^T\delta d$,
$\delta p=(w'(s)ds,k(s)\delta d+k'(s)ds\,d)$ and
$\delta Q=(I-QQ^T)\delta p/\|p\|$. Reset contributes
\[
 dP^+=G\,dC\,G^T+dG\,C\,G^T+GC\,dG^T,
 \qquad dG_\theta=[d(d\theta_{\rm inj})]_\times/2.
\]
Radial BA projection has derivative $I$ inside its ball and
$B\|b\|^{-1}(I-uu^T)$ outside. At its boundary use its finite nonexpansive
inclusion, not a nonexistent ordinary derivative. BG additionally retains the
literal inward factor, finite checks and nextafter branch. For AW, at a
nonzero spectral face $X=U\operatorname{diag}(\lambda)U^T$, the derivative of
$X_+$ is $U(L\circ U^T\delta X U)U^T$ with
$L_{ij}=(\lambda_i^+-\lambda_j^+)/(\lambda_i-\lambda_j)$ and the diagonal
limit. At zero eigenvalues use the proven positive-part Lipschitz inclusion.
No global twice-differentiable AW/reset/projection/scheduler model is asserted.

\paragraph{Raw Mahony normalization: exact rational local bound.}
The IEEE binary32 premise here is round-to-nearest with gradual underflow,
without unsafe reassociation, and represented pre-normalization squared norm
in $[1/4,4]$. Set $M=2^{23}$, $C=\texttt{0x5f375a86}$ and $u=2^{-24}$.
Power-of-four scaling reduces the positive normal inverse-square-root input
to $s\in[1,4)$. The bit seed on its three exponent strata has form
$y_0=a-bs+\epsilon$, with respectively
\[
 (a,b,\epsilon_{\max})=
 (C/(2M)-94,1/4,1/(4M)),\quad
 (C/(2M)-377/4,1/8,1/(4M)),\quad
 (C/(4M)-375/8,1/16,1/(8M)).
\]
With $d=C-190M$, the second stratum ends at
$2(1+(2d+1)/M)$ and the third starts at $2(1+(2d+2)/M)$.
The exact rational extrema of $s(a-bs+\epsilon)^2$ occur at endpoints or
$s=(a+\epsilon)/(3b)$; positivity makes it monotone in $\epsilon$.
The certificate evaluates only these analytic extrema and proves
$93/100\le z=s y_0^2\le108/100$. No mantissas are sampled.

The literal single Newton step, including rounding, satisfies
\[
 x y^2=\tfrac14 z(3-tz)^2v,\qquad
 (1-u)^2\le t\le(1+u)^2,\quad (1-u)^4\le v\le(1+u)^4.
\]
The repeated $z$ remains linked. This cubic has only an interior maximum at
$z=1/t$ in this domain; it decreases with $t$. The rational lower bound is
$\min_{z\in\{.93,1.08\}}z(3-(1+u)^2z)^2(1-u)^4/4$, and an upper bound
is $(1+u)^4/(1-u)^2$. A four-component rounded dot product has relative
error at most $\gamma_4=4u/(1-4u)$ plus the conservatively bounded gradual
underflow charge $64\,2^{-149}$ relative to the stated norm floor.
Final component products add squared relative factors $(1\pm u)^2$ and
absolute charge at most $16\,2^{-149}$. Exact rational comparison gives
\[
                  0.995<\|q_{\rm out}^{M}\|^2<1.001.
\]
This extends the pre-normalization norm lemma; it does not prove every future
input is in $[1/4,4]$, bind the pitch/integral additive caps, or close the tuner.
For a fixed seed the real normalization map is
$f(q)=y_0(3/2-y_0^2\|q\|^2/2)q$ and
\[
 Df(q)e=y(q)e-y_0^3q(q^Te),\quad
 f(q+e)-f(q)-Df(q)e
 =-y_0^3((q^Te)e+\tfrac12\|e\|^2q+\tfrac12\|e\|^2e).
\]
Its norm is at most $\tfrac32y_0^3\|q\|\|e\|^2+
\tfrac12y_0^3\|e\|^3$. A bit-seed change is a hybrid branch change and must
be enclosed, not differentiated through as a smooth map.

\paragraph{Quotient, storage and service perturbation.}
Retain the exact physical gauge $r_0=(R_y(-\psi)e_x,0,\ldots,-ge_y)$,
$J=P^{-1}$, $r=r_0/\sqrt{r_0^TJr_0}$ and $\Pi_Q=rr^TJ$.
The moving metric contributes $dJ=-J\,dP\,J$ and
$d\Pi_Q=dr\,r^TJ+r\,dr^TJ+rr^T dJ$; it is not legitimate to freeze this
projector in the coupled derivative. The exact transported relation retains
$M_Q\xi+C_Q\alpha+b_Q$ and any covariance coupling. The physical bias
curvature bound $g^2(\beta^6/36+\beta^4/4)$ gives the conditional central-chart
metric bound $\|J_{ba,ba}\|g^2(\beta^6/36+\beta^4/4)$; the actual nominal
chart needs its own transport/remainder bound. Neither a physical angle cap
nor zero total estimator variation along identical records substitutes for
that metric conversion.

For a complete literal probe word $T=L_{n-1}\cdots L_0$,
\[
 dT=\sum_i L_{n-1}\cdots L_{i+1}\,dL_i\,L_{i-1}\cdots L_0.
\]
The suffix/prefix products are formed before norms. For the physical
two-column root $B_k$, the actual probe is $\Phi_{j,k}=T_{\rm word}B_k$,
so $d\Phi_{j,k}=dT_{\rm word}B_k+T_{\rm word}dB_k$. The last term retains
source-phase changes of the physical heading/BG root. In the information
formula below, $T_{j,k}$ denotes this complete rooted probe. Each applied magnetic
term uses $Y_j=H_{m,j}T_{j,k}$ and contributes
\[
 dI_j=dY_j^T S_j^{-1}Y_j+Y_j^T S_j^{-1}dY_j
       -Y_j^TS_j^{-1}dS_jS_j^{-1}Y_j.
\]
This transports the actual service probe, not the nonlinear mean tangent.
For a finite perturbation, stack
$\bar Y_j=\bar S_j^{-1/2}\bar H_j\bar T_j$ and
$D_j=\bar S_j^{-1/2}(H_jT_j-\bar H_j\bar T_j)$.
If the complete window has $\|\bar Y\|\le b$, $\|D\|\le d$ and every
$E_j=\bar S_j^{-1/2}(S_j-\bar S_j)\bar S_j^{-1/2}$ has norm at most $e<1$,
the block-diagonal resolvent identity proves
\[
 \|I-\bar I\|\le\frac{e b^2+2bd+d^2}{1-e}.
\]
Indeed $\|(I+E)^{-1}\|\le(1-e)^{-1}$ and
$\|(I+E)^{-1}-I\|\le e/(1-e)$; expand the same stacked quadratic.
Consequently a \emph{proved} center floor $\mu_0$ would give
$\lambda_{\min}I\ge\mu_0-(e b^2+2bd+d^2)/(1-e)$.
The planar parity further gives
$I_\pm=\cos^2\theta\,I_O+\sin^2\theta\,I_E$ for the two physical probes,
where $I_O,I_E$ here use unit $R_y(-\psi)e_z,e_y$ roots, respectively,
not the scaled sum/difference columns. The original BG scaling is retained
and the cross term is zero. It preserves,
rather than discards, the odd service dynamics.

\textbf{Attempt to close the cell: OPEN.}
The derived identities do not yet yield source-uniform $\rho<1$, $c_2,c_3,d$
for a common storage, or an inherited radius. In particular the nominal
$\widehat A$, learned-reference alignment domain, gate margins and uniform center
information floor have no proven compact bounds from construction/release.
The local inverse bound alone cannot supply these. Over an enlargement that
permits rejection of every magnetic correction, the information sum is
exactly zero; failure on that enlargement is class D and is not exclusion of
the planar physical history. No value near $7.02$ is inserted as $\mu_0$.
The radius and floor fields are deliberately null, not filled from replay.
The missing next proof is a complete-word storage inequality on the causal
center's inherited reference/nominal-state domain, together with branch and
every-prefix containment. No new physical premise is adopted to supply it.

Structures preserved: both actual covariance blocks, all 21 error coordinates,
physical gauge/curvature, held and active corrections, OU/BG dependence,
Joseph/reset/projections/AW, same-history generator and clocks, learned
reference and actually applied service. Relaxations introduced: real-operation
lifts on fixed regular branches and explicit norm upper bounds after linked
algebra. No all-time service, exclusion or regional theorem is promoted.

\subsection{Private frontend binding and conditional pitch--integral tube}
The old interval frontend is not a literal implementation certificate.
\texttt{Mahony\_AHRS<float>::invSqrt} uses a bit seed and one Newton step,
not exact normalization. For $q=(1,0,0,0)$, zero gyro and aligned nonzero
acceleration, feedback vanishes but the first raw quaternion norm is not one.
\texttt{planar-service-frontend-binding.json} evaluates the exact binary32
operation word with round-to-nearest, ties-to-even and no contraction.
The pre-normalization quaternion identity remains valid; replacing the
post-normalization raw quaternion by a unit quaternion is not justified.
The vertical frontend uses its raw components, whereas its public quaternion
accessor separately normalizes them. Gravity subtraction, initial tilt seed,
readiness/period chronology, literal tuner constants and the AW clock must
also be bound. The unbound reference HistoryCell entry now fails closed.

A narrower conditional frontend calculation is already executable in
\texttt{planar\_service\_mahony\_tube.py}. For pitch error $e$ and integral
feedback $i$, put $z=(e,10i)$ and
\[
G=\begin{bmatrix}10&-5\\-5&15\end{bmatrix},\quad
A(a)=\begin{bmatrix}1-a(.2h+.02h^2)&h/10\\-10(.02)ha&1\end{bmatrix}.
\]
For $a\in[.49,.51]$, exact endpoint LDL checks and matrix convexity give
$A(a)^TGA(a)\preceq(1-.00027)G$. The proposed additive caps give
$G$-norm charge at most $7841/12000000000$, strictly less than the radial
margin $.006(.00027)/2=.00000081$. Thus those hypotheses imply retention
of $\|z\|_G\le.006$, without assuming the raw quaternion has unit norm.
The original caps alone are CONDITIONAL. The following source calculation
discharges them under explicit arithmetic qualifications; it does not certify
an arbitrary target libm, the MEKF domain, or magnetic admission.

\subsection{Inherited private-frontend domain and reference cone}
\label{app:planar-frontend-domain}
This component enters the complete finite-error inequality through reference
geometry and the one-way generator domain, never as a MEKF contraction factor.
Assume binary32 round-to-nearest with gradual underflow and no unsafe
reassociation, correctly rounded square root, and relative error at most
$2u$ for the fixed guard exponential arguments, where $u=2^{-24}$.
These are explicit implementation qualifications, not consequences of the
C++ language standard. The target-toolchain qualification is OPEN.
Delivered samples here are componentwise rounded samples of the specified
exact planar source; qualification of a different sensor/libm generator is
separate. No physical premise is strengthened.

The guard can be retained without replacing its output by an assumed raw
input. The source derivative bound in the guard certificate gives a sample
increment $d<1/3000$, including $20u$ for component rounding.
Taylor lower bounds on $\exp(x)$ and $3.14159<\pi<22/7$ give the represented
detector factor $0<\gamma<1/2$ and RMS factor
$1/1000<\beta<1/100$ under the stated exponential qualification.
The first detector state stays componentwise below $20$; its scalar LP
operation error is less than $100u$. If $e_k=a_k-s_k$ denotes its unrounded
high-pass output, then
\[
 |e_k|\le\tfrac12(|e_{k-1}|+d)+100u<1/1000
 \quad\hbox{when }|e_{k-1}|\le1/1000.
\]
The represented output is below $11/10000$. The second LP state remains below
$11/5000$, so its output is below $1/250$ componentwise. Each RMS-square
state remains in $[0,1/10000]$: the inward margin
$(1/1000)(1/10000-1/250^2)$ exceeds the roundoff charge
$20u/10000+2^{-140}$. Thus the vector RMS is strictly below $.03$,
target and seeded weight stay exactly zero, and the guard returns the raw
input at every prefix. These are all-time inductive bounds, not sampled checks.

For seeding, the two planar vector normalizations introduce at most
$\gamma_{12}=12u/(1-12u)$ relative component error, plus the separately
bounded subnormal charge. The dot product in Eigen's positive-dot
\texttt{FromTwoVectors} branch exceeds $.9997$. Its actual formulas are
$s=\sqrt{2(1+c)}$, $q_y=-v_x/s$, $q_w=s/2$; the opposite-vector/SVD branch
is not reached. Rounding these expressions contributes at most another
$\gamma_{12}$. If $d_v=d_s=\gamma_{12}$, their direction error is bounded by
\[
 .022\left(\frac{2d_v}{1-d_v}
       +d_s\frac{1+d_v}{1-d_v}\right)+2^{-130}<\gamma_{64}.
\]
Indeed $2\arctan(q_y/q_w)$ has derivative at most two in its ratio,
and the ratio magnitude is below $.011$. Before the last rounding, the
squared norm differs from one by
$(\|v\|^2-1)/(2(1+c))$; its absolute value is bounded by
$(2d_v+d_v^2)/3.9$. Including $d_s$ still gives less than $\gamma_{64}$.
The physical acceleration-angle offset is bounded by
\[
 \delta_* = \frac{(1/50)(11/35)^2}{g(39999/40001)}+4u<1/4000.
\]
Consequently the actual seed has $|e_0|<.001$, zero integral, and raw squared
norm in $(.995,1.001)$. It lies strictly inside $\|z\|_G\le.006$.
This argument applies to a literal reset and reseed if one occurs; proof-word
boundaries do not reset the observer.

Let $e_k=2\operatorname{atan2}(q_y,q_w)-\psi(t_k)$ immediately before sample
$k$ is processed, and $i_k$ be the inherited integral. Use the exact dyadic
shipping values $h=\operatorname{RN}(.005)$,
$k_p=\operatorname{RN}(.2)$, $k_i=\operatorname{RN}(.02)$.
The previous bit-seed proof extends to pre-normalization squared norm
$[1/4,128]$ by even exponent shifts; all intermediates stay normal and the
same three cubic strata apply. This covers the accelerometer, whose squared
norm is between $81$ and $100$, as well as the quaternion.
Before the final component multiplies its normalization factor $\kappa$
satisfies $.995<\kappa^2<1.001$. The linked ideal feedback is
\[
 f_k=\tfrac12\|q_k\|^2\kappa_k\sin(\delta_k-e_k)
     =a_k(\delta_k-e_k),\qquad .49<a_k<.51.
\]
The lower bound uses $\operatorname{sinc}(x)\ge1-x^2/6$ on
$|x|\le.0021+.00025$; it never normalizes the raw Mahony quaternion to one.

The source scalar DAG, including the \emph{new} integral in the corrected
gyro, now gives
\[
 z_{k+1}=A(a_k)z_k+B(a_k)\delta_k+\epsilon_k,
 \quad |\epsilon_{k,1}|<46\,10^{-9},\quad
 |\epsilon_{k,2}|<2\,10^{-9}.
\]
Here $A,B$ use the dyadic gains above. These caps are evaluated as exact
